/-!
# The numerical value of the area of Gerver's sofa

For a solution `P` of Romik's system in the box of `P.Bounds`, we enclose the six curve areas that
make up the area of Gerver's sofa (the cap `𝒥(𝐀) + 𝒥(𝐀(π/2), 𝐂(0)) + 𝒥(𝐂)` minus the niche
`𝒥(𝐱|[t₁, t₄]) - 𝒥(𝐁|[t₃, π/2]) - 𝒥(𝐃|[0, t₂])`), and deduce `ga_area_lower`: the sum is at least
`2.2` (its value is `2.21953…`).

## Method

* **Splitting.** The contact curves are piecewise `C¹` with breakpoints `t₁ = φ`, `t₂ = θ`,
  `t₃ = π/2 - θ`, `t₄ = π/2 - φ`, hence of class `C^BV` (`ga_isCBV_A`, …), and the curve area
  functional is additive (`proposition7_2_6`).
* **One phase.** On the closed phase interval `[t_{i-1}, t_i]` each curve agrees with the smooth
  phase curve of `MovingSofaOptimality.Gerver.Frame` (`gs_contactA_eq`, …), so `𝒥 = ½ ∫ Z × Z'`
  (`ga_curveArea_eq`). In the rotating frame (`ga_cross_A`, …), with `W = (w₁, w₂)`:
  `𝐀 × 𝐀' = ρ_A (w₁ + 1 + κ·u_t)`, `𝐂 × 𝐂' = ρ_C (w₂ + 1 + κ·v_t)`,
  `𝐁 × 𝐁' = (ρ_A - 1)(w₁ + κ·u_t)`, `𝐃 × 𝐃' = (1 - ρ_C)(-w₂ - κ·v_t)` and
  `𝐱 × 𝐱' = w₁β - w₂α - α κ·v_t + β κ·u_t`.
  Each integrand is `p₀(t) + p₁(t) cos t + p₂(t) sin t` with polynomials `pᵢ`; it is the derivative
  of `F(t) = P(t) + Q(t) cos t + R(t) sin t` (a `ga_TP`; `ga_TP.integral`), with coefficients
  `ga_K_*` computed by a computer algebra system and checked here by `ring`.
* **Numerics.** `F(b) - F(a)` is enclosed by interval arithmetic (`ga_num_*`, with the steps of
  `MovingSofaOptimality.Basic.Interval`), from the enclosures of `P.Bounds` (width `2·10⁻⁷`), of
  `π`, and of `cos`, `sin` at `φ`, `θ` (Taylor bounds, `cos_mem_taylor`, `sin_mem_taylor`); every
  step is an explicit enclosure checked by `norm_num`. This file is generated by
  `scripts/area/gen.py` (exact rational arithmetic, outward rounding to 12 decimals).

## Main results

* `ga_curveArea_A_mem`, `ga_curveArea_C_mem`, `ga_segArea_mem`, `ga_curveArea_x_mem`,
  `ga_curveArea_B_mem`, `ga_curveArea_D_mem`: the six enclosures (each of width `10⁻⁴` or
  `2·10⁻⁴`);
* `ga_area_lower`: `2.2 ≤ 𝒥(𝐀) + 𝒥(𝐂) + 𝒥(𝐀(π/2), 𝐂(0)) - 𝒥(𝐱|[t₁, t₄]) + 𝒥(𝐁|[t₃, π/2])
  + 𝒥(𝐃|[0, t₂])`.
-/

@[expose] public section

